from pathlib import Path
import json
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
NOTES=[]
def add(id,section,title,scans,location,kind,body,context,query,confidence=None,reason=None,refs=None):
 NOTES.append(dict(id=id,section=section,title=title,scans=scans,location=location,kind=kind,body=body,context=context,query=query,confidence=confidence,reason=reason,refs=refs or []))
S='I. Editorial remarks on SGA 7 IX (003–008)'
add('N01',S,'Henselianity and the strength of the isogeny conclusion',[3],'0(i), comments on 0.1','self-flagged',r'''“Pourquoi S hensélien ?” has a doubtful adjective, while “pourquoi seulement à isogénie près ?” explicitly distinguishes the case $\ell\ne p$. These are requests to separate the hypotheses needed to construct the objects from the hypotheses needed for an integral, rather than rational, comparison; the isolated remark does not establish a stronger theorem.''','Adds an earlier hypothesis audit to CT N38–N43 (36/073–077), and bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97; the later adic-base argument is a comparison lead, not an answer to every introductory objection.','hensélien|isogénie|adic|N97')
add('N02',S,'An unsupported cohomological remark is challenged',[3],'0(i), line14; 0(iv), line15','self-flagged',r'''The assessments “il semble clair … est un peu optimiste” and “ni fondé sur des théorèmes” explicitly deny that the cited draft passages have an adequate justification. Since the $H^i$ remark being criticized is not supplied here, its content and truth value cannot be reconstructed from the objection alone.''','Adds a direct authorial warning to the S19 comparison requested in README and RS MPL_33_002-049 §2; no matching statement of the criticized remark occurs in CT.','théorèmes|monodromie|S19|optimiste')
add('N03',S,'Semistable and stable reduction need distinct references',[3],'0(iii)','self-flagged',r'''The request “une référence ou définition pour réduction semi-stable d’une courbe” says that reference [3] uses only stable reduction terminology. A print comparison must identify both the cited definition and the operation by which one passes between the two kinds of model, without treating this editorial question as a denial of the reduction theorem.''','Adds a terminology question to README S19 and differs from CT N97 (36/073–079), which concerns an orthogonality argument, not the definition of a semistable curve.','semi-stable|stable|réduction|N97')
add('N04',S,'Epimorphism is not the definition being requested',[3],'section1, draft p2','self-flagged',r'''“épimorphisme n’est pas la définition d’isogénie” flags an omitted finiteness condition or an unstated categorical convention, and the same block proposes “quasi projectif” in place of “séparé type fini”. The underlying draft proposition is absent, so these are two local corrections to check against its exact group-scheme category, not a license to replace all finite-type hypotheses by quasi-projectivity.''','Adds source-specific comparison tasks to README S19; CT N39 (36/074) also makes finite-type and étaleness hypotheses consequential, but does not settle these corrections.','isogénie|finite-type|étales|quasi')
add('N05',S,'Comparison with the Artin–Mazur pairing is expressly unverified',[4],'draft p4, 1.3.1','self-flagged',r'''“As tu vérifié si l’accouplement d’A. M. coïncide avec le tien ?” asks for equality of constructions, not merely agreement of their ranks or perfectness. The initials remain doubtful in the transcription, but the context’s explicit Artin–Mazur title makes that expansion a strong research lead, with normalization and signs still to compare.''','Adds an earlier explicit comparison request to CT N57–N59 and its title inventory (36/082–084); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N57 and N59 without certifying their geometric identification.','Artin|Mazur|accouplement|N57')
add('N06',S,'Formal unramifiedness and separable closure are questioned',[4],'draft p4, lines4–20','self-flagged',r'''The isolated questions “formellement non ramifié ?” and “séparablement clos ?” ask which precise hypotheses or notions are intended. The following complaint about the absence of a reference makes it especially unsafe to supply a stronger smoothness, étaleness, or algebraic-closure assumption from later theory.''','Adds an unresolved definition-and-hypothesis audit to README S19; CT N38–N42 (36/073–077) concern a different lifting argument and cannot retrospectively fix these words.','formellement|unramified|séparablement|étaleness')
add('N07',S,'Strictness of the weight filtration and detection of isogenies',[4],'draft p14 paragraph','unproved claim',r'''For torus-by-abelian extensions, the note asserts that a morphism is “strictement compatible à la filtration par … poids” and is an isogeny exactly when its $T_\ell$ map is one. A proof must specify the base field, the meaning of an isogeny of Tate modules, and why a statement on the toric and abelian graded pieces controls both kernel and cokernel.''','Adds a proposed replacement argument to CT N40–N41 and N49 (36/075–079); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97, while differing from a claim that bilinear vanishing alone gives an orthogonal complement.','poids|weight|isogénie|N49')
add('N08',S,'The exact remark 2.6(a) is already under scrutiny',[4],'final paragraph, draft p20','self-flagged',r'''“S est-il un trait dans cette remarque ; sait-on assez sur le passage à la limite et Néron ?” attaches two proof obligations to Remark 2.6(a). The question is valuable because it identifies a precise draft locator at which one must check the trait reduction and compatibility of the Néron construction with the limit, rather than invoke the weight argument abstractly.''','Adds an explicit earlier criticism of the same numbered comparison cited by CT N97 (36/073–079); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97, whose reported print check remains an earlier run’s claim here.','2.6|Néron|limite|N97')
add('N09',S,'Torsion kernels, specialization and a doubtful generic subscript',[5],'draft pp23–24','unproved claim',r'''The proposed simplification ${}_nK=\ker({}_nG\to{}_nH)$ is followed by a proposed subgroup relation between specialized fibres and the question “dim Ker($u_\eta$)=0 ?”, with $s$ an alternative to $\eta$. These require the actual torsion-sheaf topology and specialization maps; the preferred generic subscript must not be changed to the special fibre because that would make a familiar argument.''','Adds a kernel-level precursor to CT N39 (36/074), which reports finite étale lifting of torsion; neither its different setup nor RS MPL_91_099-143 §68 supplies the missing specialization hypotheses.','torsion|specialization|spécialisation|étale')
add('N10',S,'Inertia outside the henselian setting',[5],'draft p28, 3.5 and inserted3.7','self-flagged',r'''“Les notions … ont elles été définies pour S non hensélien ?” is followed by the concrete requirement “choisir un groupe d’inertie”. A reconstruction should distinguish an object defined after a choice of geometric point or valuation from a choice-independent object descended to the original base.''','Adds a definitional issue to CT N38 and N45 (36/073–077,081), where adic and strictly henselian versions differ; bears on README S19, not on a new solution of an arithmetic section problem.','inertie|hensélien|strict-henselian|N45')
add('N11',S,'A tubular-neighbourhood statement is proposed in a ringed topos',[5],'draft p33ff., (4.2)','unproved claim',r'''After asking for an “énoncé style ‘voisinage tubulaire’ compréhensible”, the note describes a morphism of étale topoi and a relative scheme with a pulled-back structure sheaf. The base letter in the relative topos and the small adjoining annotation are doubtful, so establishing the direction of inverse and direct image and the relevant ring object is prior to any geometric interpretation.''','Adds an explicit topos-level formulation to README S19 and differs from CT N62–N79 (39/004,010–015), which concern formal inverse-function axioms rather than this Néron neighbourhood.','topos|formel|inverse|Néron')
add('N12',S,'The trace/contragredient correction is contested',[5],'draft p36, line−6 and pencil mark','self-flagged',r'''The live sentence says that the trace is not what is contragredient and that “l’énoncé primitif était correct”, beside a doubtful “si ?”. This records a local dispute about the direction of duality; neither the original statement nor an unambiguous resolution of the pencil response is supplied.''','Adds a source-level comparison issue to CT N53 and N57 (36/084), where transpose and geometric duality are distinguished; bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N57 only as a normalization lead.','contragr|trace|transpose|dualité')
add('N13',S,'Killed by ell versus ell-primary torsion',[5],'draft p40, (4.5.4) and following lines','self-flagged',r'''“$\ell$-groupe signifie-t-il tué par $\ell$, ou par un $\ell^n$ ?” exposes a distinction that changes the range of every finite-level statement in that paragraph. The same block asks whether the trait is strictly local and requests an identifiable “loc cit.”; none of those conventions is established by the excerpt.''','Adds an explicit terminology warning to CT N21–N26 (36/061–063,071), whose finite exponent and inverse-limit distinctions are closely related; the later notes do not answer the missing reference.','exponent|ℓ-primary|strictement|loc cit')
add('N14',S,'Dirac normalization can be checked independently',[6],'draft p41, line−2','own idea',r'''The two displayed alternatives are $f(e)=1$ and $f(e)=\#G$, both zero elsewhere. For normalized counting measure $|G|^{-1}\sum_g$, their integrals are respectively $|G|^{-1}$ and $1$, whereas ordinary counting measure reverses which is the unit-mass convention. This elementary calculation explains why a definition of the measure, rather than quotation marks alone, is needed.''','Adds a concrete normalization test to CT N14–N16 (36/066–072), which describe reused Haar-measure material; it does not identify that source with the draft criticized here.','Dirac|Haar|measure|normaliz','high','direct evaluation of the two finite sums; no geometric hypothesis is used')
add('N15',S,'The n=2 and non-strictly-local proof is distrusted',[6],'draft p42, beginning of proof 4.7','self-flagged',r'''“je n’ai pas confiance en dehors du cas strictement local” explicitly limits confidence in the proof, and the preceding words single out $n=2$. Because the criticized proof is absent, the next task is to identify its descent step and its use of a chosen splitting, rather than manufacture a counterexample from the numeral2.''','Adds an explicit proof warning to README S19; CT N30 (36/067) also isolates a local lifting argument, but concerns roots of line bundles and is not a resolution of4.7.','strictement local|lifting|n-th root|preuve')
add('N16',S,'Pro-objects or schemes is an actual categorical question',[6],'draft p46, (5.2.3.1)','self-flagged',r'''“S’agit-il de pro-objets, ou de schémas” questions both a formula and the phrase “par définition”, while the margin replies “je le dis !”. The response shows disagreement about exposition, but does not determine the intended category in this excerpt.''','Adds an earlier categorical warning to CT N25 and N47 (36/063,079–080), and to the projective-system distinction on current scans032 and050; it does not collapse those three uses of limits into one theorem.','pro-objets|projective|inverse limit|N47')
add('N17',S,'The criticism of5.7 is withdrawn locally',[6],'draft p53,5.7 and adjoining parenthesis','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The judgment that5.7 is a repetition for $\ell\ne p$ and “un peu idiot” for $\ell=p$ is crossed out, while “utilisé en5.15” remains. Both the criticism and its withdrawal occur on scan006, so the surviving annotation is evidence of a dependency, not a live rejection of the result.''','Adds a documented withdrawal to README S19; differs from CT N26 (36/062–063), where a restriction is removed through a new obstruction argument, not through this dependency note.','5.15|withdraw|restriction|p-primary')
add('N18',S,'Characteristic hypotheses must be traced through the dependency',[6],'draft56bis5.15;55ter6.6','self-flagged',r'''The note says that $\operatorname{car}(k)\ne\ell$ is no longer needed in5.15, but insists that6.6 uses $\operatorname{car}(K)=0$ “via Tate”; the margin answers that this is stated in the hypotheses of5.15. This distinguishes residue characteristic from fraction-field characteristic and asks whether a proof dependency transports the correct assumption.''','Adds an explicit dependency audit to CT N26 (36/062–063) and RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97; the later enlargement of a coefficient range does not remove the cited Tate hypothesis.','characteristic|caract|Tate|hypoth')
add('N19',S,'Divisibility and the discrete part cannot be alternated silently',[6],'draft pp57–59','self-flagged',r'''“tu supposes tantôt $A^0$ $\ell$-divisible … tantôt non” and “Que fais-tu de la partie discrète ?” flag two omitted structural distinctions. They must be tested before identifying a connected $p$-divisible part with a complete torsion object or applying a limit argument to it.''','Adds direct warnings to CT N39 and N47 (36/074,079–080), where finite torsion lifts and compatible towers are separate; also bears on the present divisibility qualifications on050.','divisib|discrète|torsion|tower')
add('N20',S,'The manuscript calls6.3 false and gives a deformation strategy',[6],'final paragraph, draft p59','self-flagged',r'''“6.3 est faux” is followed by the proposal to deform $E\times F$, with $E$ ordinary and $F$ nonordinary elliptic, to a generically simple abelian variety. The disputed assertion is the lifting of the largest ordinary abelian subvariety, and the page sketches rather than proves the required deformation and generic simplicity.''','Adds a specific claimed counterexample absent from CT; CT N39–N49 (36/074–080) concern torsion/orthogonality and do not certify or refute this algebraic lifting claim, so README S19 remains the relevant register task.','ordinaire|ordinary|simple|deform')
add('N21',S,'What would complete the proposed deformation counterexample',[6],'same final paragraph','own idea',r'''A rigorous version of “générique simple” would choose a family specializing to $E\times F$ whose geometric generic fibre has no positive-dimensional proper abelian subvariety, then show that a lift of the ordinary elliptic factor would supply precisely such a subvariety. The distinction between an algebraic abelian subvariety and a formal or $p$-divisible subgroup is essential, since the latter need not contradict generic simplicity. This supplies a proof plan conditional on the existence and specialization properties of the chosen family, not a completed counterexample.''','Adds a narrowly defined test beyond CT N39–N49 (36/074–080); no context claim settles this deformation problem, and no status change in RS is proposed.','ordinaire|deformation|formal|subgroup','medium','the contradiction from an algebraic lifted factor is clear, but the required family and extension argument have not been constructed here')
add('N22',S,'Finite and toric parts up to isogeny are an expectation',[7],'draft pp61–61bis','conjecture',r'''“Il me semble qu’il doit être possible de définir, à isogénie près” introduces finite and toric parts “ayant le bon rang”. The nearby “non, mon argument marche” records a reply, but its attachment does not make a proof of that proposed construction visible here.''','Adds an explicit expectation to CT N40–N49 (36/075–080); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97, while the rank/saturation distinction in CT N49 prevents mistaking the later vanishing argument for this construction.','partie|torique|rank|isogénie')
add('N23',S,'The limits of the editorial reading are themselves recorded',[7],'draft p60 and pp61–61bis','self-flagged',r'''“Je n’ai ps confiance en ‘il est immédiat’ de6.4” is answered “c’est pourtant trivial”, and the next entry explicitly says “Je n’ai pas lu”. These marks document an unresolved proof dispute and a stated reading boundary; the latter is not an omitted page of the present transcription.''','Adds an evidential limitation to README S19 and differs from CT N45 (36/081), whose entire cancelled draft is visible in the earlier package; neither note licenses filling an unseen argument.','immédiat|trivial|proof|S19')
add('N24',S,'The Serre reference concerns an uncertain abbreviation',[7],'draft p64/67bis and6.6','reading question',r'''The primary reading is “l’indép. de $\ell$”, with “l’insép.” retained as an alternative, followed by a complaint that6.6 is used in5.13. A bibliography search should therefore start with independence of $\ell$ while preserving the palaeographic alternative and the unresolved draft-page number.''','Adds a precise reading constraint to CT N97 (36/073–079) and README S19; no Serre citation in the supplied context resolves the abbreviation.','Serre|indep|insép|6.6')
add('N25',S,'Strict henselianity and excellence are explicit open hypotheses',[7],'draft pp87–92bis,9.4','self-flagged',r'''“S strictement hensélien ?” is justified by a later use in the draft, and “pas d’hyp. d’excellence ?” asks about a separate base condition. These should be audited independently: a reference to strict locality does not supply excellence, and neither question asserts that both hypotheses are necessary.''','Adds exact local hypothesis questions to CT N38,N42,N45 (36/073–077,081) and RS MPL_91_099-143 §68; the Greenberg/Néron excerpt does not specify an answer.','excellence|strictement|henselian|normalization')
add('N26',S,'Compatibility with R and a possible sign remain unchecked',[7],'draft p82,9.3.3; p91,9.5.8','self-flagged',r'''“compatibilité avec R ?” and the requested qualification “au signe près” leave functoriality and normalization as separate verification tasks. A comparison of perfect pairings alone cannot decide either, because an overall sign preserves perfectness.''','Adds an earlier sign warning to CT N53–N57 (36/083–084); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N57 and N59, with CT N55 offering only a later proposed sign test.','signe|sign|compatibilité|transpose')
add('N27',S,'A field enlargement and promised verifications need justification',[7],'draft p98,10.5.3–10.5.4','self-flagged',r'''“Une extension de K (qui ici est infini) est-elle requise ??” questions the construction’s field enlargement, and “Dire où on fera ces vérifications” asks for a located proof. The infinity of the field is a proposed simplification, not a demonstrated replacement for every extension used by the unseen construction.''','Adds an explicit extension-and-proof-location question to CT N48 (36/080), where K is enlarged but not renamed in a formula; the two issues are comparable but not identical.','extension de|enlarg|vérification|K′')
add('N28',S,'Commutation of the Néron model with completion needs a source',[8],'draft p101,line−6','self-flagged',r'''“Dire où on trouve que Néron commute à $S\mapsto\widehat S$” requests a specific base-change theorem. Its useful reconstruction must state the trait, residue-field, and finiteness assumptions and identify the comparison morphism, rather than treating completion as an automatic operation on all smooth models.''','Adds a concrete reference request to CT N30,N42,N52 (36/067,074–075,083); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N52 and MPL_91_099-143 §68, whose excerpts do not furnish the theorem.','completion|complet|Néron|base-change')
add('N29',S,'Extension from ell-primary torsion is stated as a question to the draft',[8],'draft p103','self-flagged',r'''After objecting to a proof left to the reader, the note paraphrases the claim that extending a biextension to $A\times A'$ is “la $\hat m$ chose” as extending it to the $\ell$-primary torsion subgroups. The assertion needs an equivalence of extension data, with its torsion and component-group hypotheses, rather than merely agreement of pairings on a Tate module.''','Adds the earlier explicit question behind CT N57 and N97 (36/084 and073–079); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N57,N97, but their reported extension obstruction is not independently established by this question.','prolong|biextension|component|7.1')
S='II. Complexes, biextensions and torsion pairings (028–034)'
add('N30',S,'The lattice–torus diagram suggests dual two-term objects',[28,29,31],'whole diagram028; complexes031','unpursued implication',r'''The labels $T=D(M')$, $T'=D(M)$ and the later complexes $[M\to G]$ organize the sheets around lattice and semiabelian data. This is a useful route to comparing dual1-motives, but the headless strokes and the left-pointing terminal arrow on028 must not be promoted into an exact-sequence assertion that the ink does not make.''','Adds a lattice–torus precursor to CT N06–N12 (36/054–058); the closest supplied RS entry, MPL_36_053-085+39 §113, is a comparison inventory rather than a theorem about1-motives.','lattice|torus|biextension|rigidification')
add('N31',S,'The extension construction uses two different internal vanishings',[29],'upper diagrams and example','unproved claim',r'''The sheet passes from maps into internal Ext to extensions by $D(M)$ and $D(M')$, and annotates the example with both a Hom-vanishing and an internal Ext-vanishing. A proof must keep these two roles separate and verify them after base change; the annotations do not justify replacing an internal sheaf by its global sections.''','Adds the construction preceding CT N06–N07 (36/054), which likewise distinguish sheaf-Hom rigidity from global classification; bears on README S19, with no supplied reviewed result closing this step.','sheaf|global|Hom|Ext')
add('N32',S,'Compatibility of the two splittings is the surviving criterion',[29],'final live paragraph after cancellation','unproved claim',r'''The live criterion says that two lifts correspond exactly when the two induced splittings “coïncident” on $\mathcal E_{M,M'}$. The longer crossed formulation should be read as a discarded presentation, while the surviving compatibility is the datum a descent construction must use.''','Adds an explicit double-rigidification compatibility to CT N07–N08 (36/054–056); the later additivity claim is related but does not replace the equality of splittings required here.','splitting|trivialisation|rigidification|coïncid')
add('N33',S,'The derived biextension class is proposed beside pourquoi',[31],'complexes, canonical class and aborted proof','self-flagged',r'''The passage replaces an ordinary quotient by complexes in degrees−1,0 and proposes a “classe canonique” in $\operatorname{Ext}^1(A\otimes^L A',G_m)$. The aborted “Soit en effet” and marginal doubtful “pourquoi ?” leave the construction or its justification visibly unfinished; one should not read the surrounding notation as a completed proof.''','Adds a direct homological construction problem to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); no earlier context result proves this class exists with the stated rigidifications.','Ext|canonique|biextension|pourquoi')
add('N34',S,'A precise published comparison for the two-term construction',[29,31,33],'compatible splittings029; Ext class031; pairing033','unpursued implication',r'''Deligne (1974, §10.2.1, p. 60) defines biextensions of two-term complexes using two compatible trivializations and identifies their degree0 and1 groups with derived-tensor Ext. This is a precise comparator for “coïncident” on029 and “jouant le rôle d’une biextension” on031; it does not date these sheets or show that their incomplete argument was already complete.''','Adds an independently checked primary comparison to README S19 and differs from CT N06–N08 (36/054–056), whose subject is rigidity of torsor descriptions; bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 §115 as additional bibliography.','derived|complex|trivialis|S19',refs=['P1'])
add('N35',S,'An explicit model tests the torsion exact sequence',[31],'definition of the n-torsion object and first exact sequence','own idea',r'''For $A=[M\xrightarrow{u}G]$, define $V_n=\{(m,g):u(m)=ng\}/\{(nm',u(m'))\}$ in the chosen sheaf category. If ${}_nM=0$ and multiplication by $n$ on $G$ is an epimorphism, projection to $m\bmod n$ gives $0\to{}_nG\to V_n\to M/nM\to0$, directly checking the two hypotheses written on031. Comparing $V_n$ with the manuscript’s internal $\operatorname{Ext}^0(\mathbb Z/n,A)$ still requires the same derived-degree convention, rather than a silent definition change.''','Adds a concrete verification method to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); no context note supplies this torsion model, and README S19 is the relevant plan entry.','torsion|Ext|exact|complex','high','kernel and local surjectivity follow directly from the displayed presentation under the two stated assumptions')
add('N36',S,'The limit sequence needs its own exactness check',[31],'final two Tate sequences','unproved claim',r'''“passant à la limite sur $n=\ell^\nu$” turns the finite sequences into Tate sequences without a separate limit argument. One must state whether this is an exact sequence of systems, sheaves, or inverse limits of sections and check the corresponding lifting condition; the first term in the second row is frozen as $T_\ell(A'|G')$, so its expected $G'$ is only a reading alternative.''','Adds a further instance to CT N25 and N43 (36/063,076–077), which already flag transition maps and uniformity; it restates the need for such control without transferring their geometric hypotheses.','inverse limit|transition|all torsion|uniform')
add('N37',S,'The local maps in the pairing construction must glue',[33],'opening triangle and following sentence','unproved claim',r'''The local maps from the torsion objects into the complexes are followed by the doubtful words “qui se recollent” and a claimed global homomorphism. The proof obligation is independence of the local lifts, compatibility on overlaps, and the precise passage from an Ext class to the displayed Hom group, not merely the existence of local representatives.''','Adds a descent problem to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056), where rigidity could control uniqueness; it does not establish the needed vanishing solely from the later wording “est discrète”.','local|global|discrète|rigidity')
add('N38',S,'The induced graded pairings are asserted without calculation',[33],'orthogonality proposition and corollary','unproved claim',r'''The orthogonality statements are followed by “sont ceux qu’on devine” for the induced lattice–torus and abelian pairings, then a perfectness corollary with explicit torsion and divisibility assumptions. The small parenthetical torsion indices are doubtful, so the task is to verify each graded pairing and exact sequence before invoking the corollary, not to repair its hypotheses by expectation.''','Adds a filtered-duality calculation to CT N49 (36/073–079), which distinguishes vanishing from equality; bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97, but the present perfectness claim has different data.','orthogonal|perfect|rank|saturation')
add('N39',S,'A filtered proof would isolate what perfectness still needs',[33],'same corollary','own idea',r'''If the two torsion objects have finite filtrations with the stated opposite orthogonality and perfect pairings on matching graded pieces, exactness of the appropriate duality functor gives perfectness of the total pairing by induction on the filtration. This reduces the corollary to the explicit graded identifications and exactness hypotheses, which remain to be checked in the source’s sheaf category. It does not derive equality of orthogonal complements from vanishing alone.''','Adds a proof organization to CT N49 and differs from the component-lattice construction of CT N54 (36/084); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97 only through the common orthogonality issue.','filtration|perfect|orthogonal complement|N54','high','the filtered isomorphism argument is formal once exact duality and the graded isomorphisms are assumed')
add('N40',S,'The typed pairing text has its own reading order',[30,32,34],'section2 across reused sheets','reading question',r'''The title “Accouplements définis par une biextension” on034 begins the typed sequence, continued on032 and then030. The archive scan order is preserved in the transcription, but a mathematical comparison must follow034→032→030 and acknowledge that030 stops at “Or cette compatibilité résulte de”.''','Adds an internal order distinct from CT’s reused-type pagination inventory (36/053,055,057,059,066–072); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 §111 and §113 without inferring a composition chronology.','reused|pagination|order|typed')
add('N41',S,'The asymmetric compatibility is stronger than the doubled one',[30,32],'(2.3.1)–(2.3.5)','unproved claim',r'''The live text explicitly removes a restriction on $Q$ for the asymmetric identity $\phi^n(mx,y)=\phi^{n'}(x,y)$, and uses it to recover the doubly multiplied formula. The displayed membership $y\in{}_{n'}P(S)$ on032 and the mismatched indices on030 are retained literally; proving the compatibility requires independently matching each domain and the Tor boundary maps.''','Adds the finite-level mechanism needed beside CT N47 (36/079–080), which records nonvanishing before taking limits; the context does not repair the printed P/Q or n/n′ discrepancies.','finite-level|Tor|compatib|pairing')
add('N42',S,'A proof formerly left to the reader is promised explicitly',[34,32,30],'034 deletion and insertion;032–030 proof','withdrawn in the same text',r'''On034 “qu’on laisse au lecteur le soin d’établir” is deleted and replaced by “cf.2.3 ci-dessous pour une démonstration”. Scans032 and030 provide the stronger compatibility and a Tor-based proof route, although the retained endpoint is incomplete. This is a withdrawal of a proof deferral across034→032→030, not a retraction of the compatibility statement.''','Adds a documented editorial repair to CT N31 (36/067,069), where proof routes also change; bears on README S19 and differs from simply counting every cancelled proof as a withdrawn theorem.','lecteur|démonstration|proof route|withdraw')
add('N43',S,'Tate notation expressly denotes a projective system here',[32,50],'definition(2.2.5); boxed NB050','unpursued implication',r'''Scan032 defines $T_\ell(M)=({}_{\ell^\nu}M)_\nu$ as a “système projectif”, and050 insists on retaining finite-level pairings when stronger divisibility is unavailable. This distinction connects the construction of the pairing to the later warnings about what can disappear after passing to a limit, without identifying a strict diagram of groups, an object in a pro-category, and its inverse-limit group.''','Adds explicit categorical language to CT N25 and N47 (36/063,079–080); bears on README S19 and qualifies any use of CT’s “projective-limit pairing” as complete finite-level data.','système projectif|projective-limit|inverse limit|finite-level')
S='III. Picard duality, adjunction and characteristic-p comparisons (035–046)'
add('N44',S,'The cohomology table does not state its hypotheses',[36],'ell row and Jacobian pairing','unproved claim',r'''The table invokes “l’autodualité de P” and “la dualité de Kummer-Serre”, but this sheet supplies no condition on $X$, its dimension, its base field, or the topology. Its Jacobian wording strongly suggests a curve calculation; that inference is a research interpretation, and cannot turn the three-row table into a theorem for every proper scheme.''','Adds a cohomological precursor to CT N57–N59 and its Artin–Mazur cover (36/082–084); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N59, with the curve hypotheses of that comparison still distinct.','Jacob|Kummer|autodualité|curve')
add('N45',S,'Three characteristic-p coefficient objects must stay separate',[36],'p row and bottom Frobenius row','reading question',r'''The frozen coefficient forms are $\zeta_p$, $\mathbb Z/p$, and a dotted $i_p$ with $\alpha_p$ as an alternative, and the left middle cell literally repeats $H^2(X,\mathbb Z/p)$. A useful comparison tests the Kummer, Artin–Schreier, and Frobenius-kernel sequences separately, retaining the distinction between flat and étale cohomology (The Stacks Project, 2026a, tag 0A3J; The Stacks Project, 2026e, tag 03PK). This can constrain a mathematical interpretation but cannot identify the uncertain glyph or correct the repeated formula after the freeze.''','Adds a coefficient-and-topology warning to CT N24–N26 (36/063), whose p-primary Brauer correction also depends on the chosen sequence; no CT reading settles the three glyphs.','Kummer|Brauer|p-primary|coefficient',refs=['P2','P3'])
add('N46',S,'The local-to-global sketch uses an exclamation mark',[37],'top Ext diagram and programme list','reading question',r'''The upper diagram writes $f_!$, while the programme below includes “Dualité de Cartier” and a highly uncertain final question ending “??”. A modern adjunction might suggest $Rf_*$ in one interpretation, but the visible exclamation mark and the separate star notation on043 remain frozen; the correct functors and ambient categories are research questions.''','Adds a functor-level ambiguity to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); bears on README S19 and differs from simply substituting the modern adjoint expected from the formula.','adjoint|Cartier|Ext|functor')
add('N47',S,'The assertion that H¹ with abelian coefficients is torsion comes first',[39],'opening line after X and A','unproved claim',r'''For “X propre et simple”, the page begins with $H^1(X,A)=\varinjlim_n{}_nH^1(X,A)$. This torsion assertion is an input to the subsequent limit comparison, and its meaning depends on the topology, dimension, and whether one is taking absolute cohomology or a relative sheaf; none of those refinements is proved here.''','Adds an abelian-coefficient torsion question distinct from CT N17–N29 (36/061–065), which concern finite multiplicative coefficients and vertical divisors; bears on README S19 without identifying the two H1 groups.','H¹|H1|torsion|Picard')
add('N48',S,'The Albanese substitution needs constants and base data',[39],'first two short exact sequences and (*)','unproved claim',r'''The Kummer sequence is rewritten using $\operatorname{Hom}(\operatorname{Alb}_X,A)$ and then $\operatorname{Hom}(A',\operatorname{Pic}^0_X)$. The treatment of constant maps, a possible Albanese torsor in the absence of a chosen point, and sheafification over the base must be specified before these substitutions can be used in a proof.''','Adds a base-data question to CT N29 (36/065), where a degree-one0-cycle has a stated role in Picard comparison; bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N59 only as a related hypothesis audit.','Albanese|zero-cycle|0-cycle|Picard')
add('N49',S,'The displayed Q and lower-dot Tate symbol remain unresolved',[39],'two boxed limit sequences','reading question',r'''Both limit displays use $\mathbb Q$, without a written quotient by $\mathbb Z$, and $T$ carries an uncertain lower dot. An extracted statement needs an independent definition of that torsion/limit object and the transition maps before deciding whether rationalization, a divisible quotient, or some other construction was intended.''','Adds a notation constraint to CT N25 and N36 (36/062–063,069,071), which warn against reconstructing limit maps and definitions from familiar symbols; CT does not supply a reading of this dot.','tensor|limit|notation|transition')
add('N50',S,'Picard zero, tau and the full functor cannot be interchanged',[39,40],'(*),(**), final Albanese diagram','reading question',r'''The comparison moves among $\operatorname{Pic}^0$, the full Picard functor, and a superscript read as $\tau|2$ on039 and more clearly $\tau$ on040. A proof must identify the maps between these functors and account for their discrete torsion quotients; the more legible later sign does not authorize replacing every earlier zero by tau.''','Adds an earlier instance of precisely the distinction emphasized by CT N58 (36/061–071,083); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N52,N57,N59, without asserting those later kernels coincide with this Ext group.','Pic|tau|P^|Néron–Severi')
add('N51',S,'The extension to singular X is explicitly open',[40],'margin beside the canonical isomorphism','self-flagged',r'''“Cet isomorphisme reste-t-il valable pour X singulier ???” is followed by a tentative need for $H^i(X,G_m)=0$ for $i\geq2$ or $i>2$. The statement is a question about the obstruction to extending the displayed duality, not a proved vanishing theorem for nonsingular schemes.''','Adds a singularity/obstruction question to CT N24,N30,N37,N58 (36/063,067,065,083), where Brauer and factoriality issues occur; bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N59 with different hypotheses.','singular|Brauer|factorial|vanish')
add('N52',S,'A smooth point already tests the broad vanishing reading',[40],'same marginal vanishing question','own idea',r'''Under the $i\geq2$ and absolute étale-cohomology reading, $X=\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb R$ is a smooth proper0-dimensional test over $\mathbb R$, yet $H^2(X,G_m)=\mathbb R^*/N_{\mathbb C/\mathbb R}\mathbb C^*\simeq\mathbb Z/2$. Thus nonsingularity alone cannot supply that vanishing without additional field/topology assumptions; this test does not address a hidden algebraically closed base, the alternative $i>2$, or the duality assertion itself. The Brauer–cohomology identification is the comparison used here (The Stacks Project, 2026b, tag 03R1).''','Adds an elementary diagnostic to CT N24 and N58 (36/063,061–071,083), which already treat a Brauer obstruction; it does not refute their more specific curve or base hypotheses.','Brauer|vanish|field|hypoth','high','the norm from C has image R-positive, giving the displayed two-element quotient; the interpretation tested is explicitly restricted',refs=['P6'])
add('N53',S,'The affine Cartier-duality comparison needs a defined dual category',[40],'boxed affine-group formula','unproved claim',r'''The formula $H^1(X,G)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}(D(G),\underline{\operatorname{Pic}}_X)$ is asserted for “G commutatif affine”. Its scope depends on whether $D(G)$ is a finite Cartier dual, a formal group, or a sheaf on a larger site; an ordinary scheme of characters need not represent the same functor.''','Adds a dual-category requirement to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056) and differs from CT N59 (36/083–084), whose specific Jacobian pairing does not establish an all-affine-group formula.','Cartier|affine|dual|sheaf')
add('N54',S,'The additive group is a useful test of the chosen dual functor',[40],'same affine formula','own idea',r'''Test the comparison with $G=G_a$ over a characteristic-zero field before interpreting $D$ by characters on field-valued points: a homomorphism $G_a\to G_m$ over that field is trivial because an invertible polynomial is constant. That calculation alone does not make the sheaf dual zero on nilpotent test schemes, so a potential formal dual must be retained when comparing with $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)$. The test separates two meanings of dual rather than asserting that the manuscript’s formula is false.''','Adds a concrete category test to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); no supplied context statement decides the formal representability of D(G_a).','additive|formal|Hom|Cartier','high','the field-valued character calculation is elementary, while the warning explicitly avoids extrapolating it to all test schemes')
add('N55',S,'The Hom/cohomology comparison is marked with an affine question',[41],'first enclosure and following text','self-flagged',r'''The first comparison is qualified by “quand tout est affine sur S (?)”. The question is whether cohomology commutes with the indicated group-Hom functor in that relative setup, and affineness by itself should not be substituted for the exactness or acyclicity condition a proof would actually use.''','Adds an explicit hypothesis question to CT N06–N07 (36/054), where vanishing hypotheses control another Hom/Ext comparison; bears on README S19, with no direct answer in the context.','affine|Hom|cohomology|vanishing')
add('N56',S,'The Ext/cohomology exchange asks for its conditions',[41,44],'second box041; unfinished adjunction sketches044','self-flagged',r'''“Sous quelles conditions ???” follows the proposed exchange between $H^1$ and $\underline{\operatorname{Ext}}^1$, and044 repeats unfinished versions. A construction of a comparison morphism must be distinguished from an isomorphism, with the extra terms or differentials identified in the relevant derived-functor spectral sequences.''','Adds the open exchange problem preceding CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); the later rigidification statements give possible special-case hypotheses, not the general conditions asked for here.','spectral|Ext|conditions|rigidification')
add('N57',S,'The birational Picard argument is a cancelled draft',[42],'entire text and diagram','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The whole argument for a proper birational morphism is crossed out, including the assertion about the complement having codimension at least2 and the unfinished passage from a line bundle to a divisor cycle. The cancellation on042 is not accompanied by a replacement proof on that sheet, so it supplies a proposed route to compare with later Cartier-extension arguments, not a live theorem.''','Adds a cancelled birational route to CT N37 (36/065,083), whose explicit local-factoriality observation is a narrower live comparison; bears on README S19 and does not strengthen that later observation.','birational|factorial|Cartier|codimension')
add('N58',S,'The degree-two line deliberately does not give an equality',[43],'i=2 line with exclamation mark','self-flagged',r'''The line for $H^2(X,D(G))$ has $\ne$, with an exclamation mark below it, before the sum of an Ext term and a higher-Picard Hom term. It therefore cannot be quoted as a direct-sum formula; differentials and extension data would have to be controlled before any such decomposition could be established.''','Adds an explicit warning to CT N24–N25 (36/063), which retain a higher obstruction and its transitions; bears on README S19 and differs from a theorem identifying all higher terms.','higher|Brauer|spectral|obstruction')
add('N59',S,'A truncation calculation could organize the higher Picard terms',[37,40,43],'Ext sketch037; singularity question040; Pic-dot043','own idea',r'''In a chosen site where derived adjunction applies, set $K=Rf_*G_m$ and compare $\tau_{\leq1}K$ with $K$ by its truncation triangle. Applying $R\operatorname{Hom}(G,-)$ gives a precise place for contributions from $R^2f_*G_m$ and higher sheaves, and the spectral sequence $\operatorname{Ext}^p(G,R^qf_*G_m)\Rightarrow\operatorname{Ext}^{p+q}(f^*G,G_m)$ supplies a test for the marked degree-two failure. Identifying this abutment with the manuscript’s cohomology and its shifted abelian formula is an additional duality step, not part of this formal calculation.''','Adds a proposed obstruction computation to CT N24,N58 (36/063,061–071,083) and RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N57; it differs from treating a Brauer correction or an Ext edge map as an automatic isomorphism.','spectral|Brauer|higher|Ext','medium','the truncation method is formal under stated derived-adjunction and convergence assumptions, but the manuscript’s cohomological identification remains unproved',refs=['P4'])
add('N60',S,'The shifted abelian line and shriek notation are separate questions',[43],'two adjunction formulas and final G-abelian line','reading question',r'''The page uses $f^!$, $f_!$, and later $f^*$ distinctly, and its final abelian line has $H^{i-1}(X,G')$ while some Ext indices remain doubtful. A derived interpretation must explain the shift and define the shriek operations instead of silently replacing every sign by the modern pullback/direct-image adjunction.''','Adds a precise notation constraint to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); bears on README S19 and is not resolved by the later use of degree1 Ext in a different rigidity problem.','Ext|degree|adjoint|rigidity')
add('N61',S,'Adjunction and relative connectedness are only sketches here',[44],'definition and bottom pointed-Hom sequence','unproved claim',r'''The definition of adjoint functors stops at “s’il existe un”, and the lower $\Pi^0_{X/S}$ and pointed-Hom sequence are likewise incomplete. One promising reconstruction is to separate maps pulled back from $S$ from maps vanishing at a section, but constancy of maps into $\gamma$ needs a connectedness or rigidity hypothesis not stated on this page.''','Adds a precursor to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056), where function-Hom vanishing is explicitly part of the problem; the later conditions should be compared, not inserted into this unfinished definition.','pointed|section|rigidity|Hom')
add('N62',S,'Fixed elements replace the first stable-part pairing',[45],'cancelled middle boxes and live final formulas','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The first pairing with $H^1(A,\mathbb W)_s$ and the Hom formula with $\Lambda$ are cancelled, while the live perfect pairing uses Frobenius-fixed $H^1(A,\mathbb W)^{(\varphi)}$ and the last stable-part Hom formula uses $W_\infty(k)$. These changes on045 distinguish an integral fixed lattice from a stable object after coefficient extension; they are not interchangeable notations.''','Adds a local correction absent from CT; CT N26 (36/062–063) also distinguishes coefficient variants, but does not establish this Witt comparison or define its stable subscript.','Witt|Frobenius|coefficient|stable')
add('N63',S,'The Artin–Schreier comparison has a base-field term',[45],'first two H1 formulas','own idea',r'''The Artin–Schreier long exact sequence contains $H^0(A,\mathcal O_A)/(F-1)$ before $H^1(A,\mathbb Z/p)$, so the displayed identification with Frobenius-fixed $H^1(A,\mathcal O_A)$ needs that quotient to vanish (The Stacks Project, 2026a, tag 0A3J). For an abelian variety over $\mathbb F_p$ with its origin, the base-field class survives by the split pullback from $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb F_p$, showing why an algebraically closed base or geometric-cohomology convention matters. This tests an unstated hypothesis and does not refute the geometric version.''','Adds a specific missing-term test to CT N24–N26 (36/063), whose coefficient change likewise requires additional terms; no context note supplies the base field for045.','Artin|coefficient|field|exact sequence','high','exactness gives the term, and the origin supplies a left inverse to base pullback',refs=['P3'])
add('N64',S,'Passing from finite Witt levels needs transition control',[45],'W_n formula and final W-infinity formula','unproved claim',r'''The sheet moves from finite $p^n$ coefficients to $\mathbb Z_p$ and then to $W_\infty(k)$ without stating a theorem on inverse limits, continuous Hom, or commutation with Frobenius invariants. Freeness and perfectness therefore require a finite-level proof together with the exact transition and base-field hypotheses.''','Restates the transition-map obligation of CT N25 (36/063) in a different Witt setting, and adds its continuous-Hom aspect; that prior counterexample is not re-derived here.','Witt|continuous|inverse limit|transition')
add('N65',S,'Covering statements need a geometric fundamental group and an origin',[45,46],'covering assertions and Tate identifications','unproved claim',r'''Scan045 says that every covering of $A$ is an abelian variety, while046 explicitly says “revêtement non ramifié”. A precise statement must specify finite connected étale coverings, the geometric base and a point over the origin before using the induced group structure and identifying the relevant pro-primary fundamental group with the Tate module.''','Adds hypotheses to CT N27–N28 and N48 (36/063–064,080), which concern covers and field enlargement of different sorts; it does not identify their connected ramified cover of a trait with an étale cover of A.','cover|revêtement|geometric|point')
add('N66',S,'A n’a pas de torsion must remain a reading question',[46],'conditional sentence before A-star torsion','reading question',r'''The text literally says “Si on admet que A n’a pas de torsion”, although $A$ was introduced as an abelian variety. The surrounding Picard calculation suggests checking whether the intended object was a Néron–Severi quotient or another notation, but neither that mathematical expectation nor the later context changes the frozen letter A.''','Adds a hypothesis/reading problem to CT N21,N32,N58 (36/061–062,065–071,083), which distinguish torsion of several Picard-related quotients; CT supplies no direct reading of this letter.','torsion|Néron–Severi|quotient|Picard')
add('N67',S,'Finite-level perfectness is a further assertion',[46],'last three displayed pairings','unproved claim',r'''The claimed Tate-module duality is followed by quotients modulo $\ell^k$ and a pairing of finite torsion objects. The identifications $T_\ell(A)/\ell^k\simeq A[\ell^k]$ and the corresponding coefficient identification require the relevant divisibility and geometric-point conventions, and should not be inferred for arbitrary sheaves from the shape of the final formula.''','Adds the hypotheses needed to relate this duality to CT N47 (36/079–080), which expressly separates finite-level nonvanishing from limit vanishing; the two claims are compatible rather than contradictory.','finite-level|Tate|divisib|pairing')
S='IV. Reconstruction from torsion and rigidity of torsors (047–052)'
add('N68',S,'The finite-level reconstruction is stated as a categorical equivalence',[48],'live statement before the cancelled proof','unproved claim',r'''Under $nQ=Q$ and surjectivity ${}_nP\to P''$, the assertion “Je dis que le foncteur … est une équiv. de catégories” classifies a biextension by its restriction and a compatible torsion pairing. A proof must establish essential surjectivity and the full morphism condition, whose live definition itself stops at “tel que $\varphi\sim$”.''','Adds the reconstruction problem preceding CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); the later rigidity statements do not supply the missing categorical proof or complete the unfinished morphism clause.','équivalence|biextension|rigid|morphism')
add('N69',S,'One vanishing in the cancelled proof has a short conditional check',[48],'cancelled (*) and following explanation','own idea',r'''The hypotheses imply $nP''=0$, since $P''$ is a quotient of ${}_nP$, and hence $P''\otimes Q=0$ when $nQ=Q$: locally write $q=nq'$ and obtain $p''\otimes q=np''\otimes q'=0$. This verifies the tensor-vanishing mechanism stated in the crossed argument, without restoring that argument to live status. The remaining lifting and Tor comparison are additional steps, especially at the level of categories rather than isomorphism classes.''','Adds a small verified algebraic component to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); no supplied RS result settles the reconstruction, and no claim of a new theorem is made.','Tor|tensor|vanish|category','high','the calculation uses only the two displayed hypotheses and local lifting through an epimorphism of sheaves')
add('N70',S,'Cancellation leaves the proof route, not a second established proof',[48],'large crossed proof block','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The long proof invokes full faithfulness, a surjectivity condition marked(**), a reference to “c)”, and finally $\operatorname{Ext}^1(P''\otimes^LQ,G)$ and Tor. Because that block is cancelled on048 and c) is not supplied as a live numbered hypothesis, it records a withdrawn route rather than a proof that may be silently reattached to the equivalence.''','Adds a proof-state distinction comparable to CT N31 and N45 (36/067,069,081); differs from treating every cancelled route as a retraction of its surrounding theorem.','cancel|withdraw|proof route|Tor')
add('N71',S,'The Pi-primary corollary continues across an unrelated verso',[48,50],'Corollaire1 at foot048 and opening050','unproved claim',r'''The corollary replaces the fixed integer by a set $\Pi$ of primes and assumes that $\varinjlim{}_nP\to P''$ is surjective and each relevant prime divides $Q$ surjectively. Its continuation is on050, not the intervening Čech typescript049, and the claimed equivalence must preserve the compatibilities among all finite-level pairings.''','Adds a concrete all-level reconstruction to CT N43,N47 (36/076–080), where uniformity and finite/limit distinctions already matter; it does not identify those geometric hypotheses with this abstract sheaf criterion.','all torsion|finite-level|transition|uniform')
add('N72',S,'The two-variable argument uses asymmetric divisibility',[50],'second Corollaire1 and proof','unproved claim',r'''The live hypotheses use $pP=P$ and $pQ'=Q'$ “ou le symétrique”, followed by torsion-surjectivity in both quotient sequences. The proof applies the one-variable result first to $(P,Q')$ and then in the opposite variable to $(P,Q)$, so the precise asymmetric divisibilities, rather than a blanket assertion that every term is divisible, are what must be checked.''','Adds an iterated reconstruction route to CT N07–N08 (36/054–056), where transposition also enters; the later additivity proposition is a different statement and does not prove this iteration.','transpos|additiv|divisib|biextension')
add('N73',S,'The boxed qualifications restrict the module description',[50],'two boxed marginal notes','self-flagged',r'''The first NB insists on “syst. projectifs”, and the second says, in a doubtful reading, that the description “en modules” needs additional $\Pi$-divisibility; otherwise one must keep the ${}_nP\times{}_nQ\to{}_nG$ system. The equalities in the first box and parts of its syntax are among the less secure readings, but the explicit instruction to retain finite levels is clear enough to block an unconditional inverse-limit reformulation.''','Adds an authorial qualification to CT N25 and N47 (36/063,079–080); it agrees with their warning about limits, while specifying a categorical description not present in their examples.','modules|projective|inverse limit|finite-level')
add('N74',S,'A bounded factor can erase a nonzero pairing at the limit',[48,50],'finite-level equivalence and boxed NB','own idea',r'''For ordinary abelian groups take $P=\mathbb Z/\ell$, $Q=G=\mathbb Q_\ell/\mathbb Z_\ell$ and $P'=0$, so $T_\ell(P)=0$, although $\operatorname{Ext}^1(P\otimes^LQ,G)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}(\mathbb Z/\ell,G)\ne0$. With a generator of $Q[\ell^r]$ represented by $1/\ell^r$, the bilinear maps $\phi_r(x,y/\ell^r)=xy/\ell\bmod\mathbb Z$ are nonzero and satisfy the simultaneous multiplication compatibility, yet induce zero on inverse limits. This supplies an explicit algebraic model for the lost finite-level data; identifying these classes with biextensions uses the derived-tensor comparison, and does not establish the full manuscript equivalence.''','Adds a reconstruction-specific example beyond CT N47 (36/079–080) and differs from CT N25’s constant-system obstruction; bears on README S19, with the earlier phenomena retained as already reported leads.','finite-level|inverse limit|Tor|N47','high','a two-term resolution computes the displayed Ext, and the finite-level formulas can be checked directly',refs=['P1'])
add('N75',S,'Group-Hom rigidity and pointed-map rigidity are different assumptions',[52],'parts(a) and(b)','unpursued implication',r'''Part(a) assumes vanishing of internal group Hom, whereas part(b) uses the stronger pointed-function condition explained as maps “pas nécessairemt additifs”. These control different automorphisms and comparisons, so identifying the two assumptions would obscure which step permits forgetting an extension structure.''','Adds the missing preceding distinction to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056), which explicitly warn about function-Hom versus group-Hom; bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 §113 as a substantive continuation, not a new settled register result.','function-Hom|group|pointed|automorphism')
add('N76',S,'The first rigidity comparison is a low-degree edge map',[52],'part(a), first displayed isomorphism','own idea',r'''Interpreting the ungraded internal Ext symbol as the degree1 sheaf, the local-to-global Ext five-term sequence has $H^1(S,\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}(A,G))$ and $H^2(S,\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}(A,G))$ on either side of the edge map. Their vanishing proves the displayed $\operatorname{Ext}^1\simeq H^0(\underline{\operatorname{Ext}}^1)$ once that spectral sequence is in place; the ringed-space version is recorded in The Stacks Project (2026d, tag 0BQP). Applying it to all slices requires the internal vanishing and the corresponding slice comparison, not only zero global Hom.''','Adds a formal proof explanation to CT N06 (36/054); it restates that note’s sheaf/global warning while supplying the precise low-degree mechanism, conditional on the intended Ext notation.','sheaf|global|Ext|vanishing','high','the two outer terms of the five-term sequence vanish with the internal Hom sheaf',refs=['P4'])
add('N77',S,'The base section explains the H¹ product decomposition',[52],'part(b), H1 decomposition','own idea',r'''Assume the written condition $f_*G_A\simeq G$ and use the zero section $e$ of $f$: then $e^*f^*=1$ makes $H^2(S,G)\to H^2(A,G_A)$ injective, forcing the Leray transgression from $H^0(S,R^1f_*G_A)$ to vanish. The resulting short exact sequence for $H^1$ splits by $e^*$, and its kernel describes classes trivial on the section. Uniqueness of a chosen rigidification up to pointed isomorphism still uses the stronger pointed-map vanishing, which should not be absorbed into the splitting alone.''','Adds a derivation of the preceding torsor comparison to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); no status of a supplied research return is changed.','rigidif|section|spectral|H¹','high','the section splits pullback in cohomology, and exactness then kills the transgression')
add('N78',S,'Full faithfulness includes the absence of automorphisms',[52],'forgetful-functor paragraph','unproved claim',r'''The forgetful functor is asserted to be “pl. fid. (de catégories discrètes)”, followed by an injection on Ext classes. The categorical claim is stronger than that injection: a proof must show that pointed isomorphisms of the underlying torsors respect the group laws and that no nontrivial pointed automorphism remains.''','Adds the preceding categorical assertion to CT N06–N08 (36/054–056); it restates their discreteness issue with a specific source and does not equate full faithfulness with essential surjectivity.','discrète|automorphism|faithful|rigidified')
add('N79',S,'Rigidity can force coherence of a primitive torsor law',[52],'essential-image criterion using pi-star and projections','own idea',r'''Under universal vanishing of pointed maps $A\to G$, the same vanishing holds for $A^r$ by subtracting the value at zero in one coordinate at a time. For a rigidified torsor with an isomorphism $\pi^*L\simeq\operatorname{pr}_1^*L\otimes\operatorname{pr}_2^*L$, normalize that isomorphism at the origin; its two associativity composites and its transpose then differ by pointed maps from $A^3$ or $A^2$ to $G$, so are equal. This gives a route from the primitive class condition to a unique commutative extension law, provided the vanishing is genuinely internal and base-change stable.''','Adds a coherence argument addressing CT N07–N08 (36/054–056); it does not claim that all birigidified torsors are biextensions without the stronger hypotheses that those notes retain.','coherence|additiv|rigidification|bitorsor','high','the comparison maps are torsor automorphisms, and universal pointed-map vanishing forces each normalized difference to be zero')
add('N80',S,'The NS sequence leads directly into the later diagonal programme',[52],'bottom exact sequence and NS quotient','unpursued implication',r'''The map from $R^1f_*G_A$ to symmetric birigidified torsors records the defect of additivity, with internal Ext as its proposed kernel and NS as the quotient. This is the preceding structural data for the later diagonal and doubling constructions, so verifying the exact sequence and the meaning of NS is a prerequisite to using their claimed splitting.''','Adds the earlier input to CT N08–N12 (36/054–058), especially the unproved biextension hypothesis and canonical doubling; differs from re-deriving CT N12’s already stated conditional division-by2 consequence.','NS|doubling|diagonal|splitting')
add('N81',S,'The lower B/A argument stays frozen despite the continuation',[52],'last row of the bottom diagram','reading question',r'''The lower symmetric-birigidified-torsor argument is frozen as $B|A$, and the vertical sign as $\wr|i$, although the preceding row uses $(A,A)$. CT’s later diagonal construction on $A\times A$ makes A a natural interpretation to investigate, but that is precisely a context-induced reading question, not permission to change the primary B reading.''','Differs at the reading level from the A×A description in CT N08–N12 (36/054–058); adds the uncertainty that must remain visible when connecting those notes to this diagram.','A×A|A\\times|diagonal|birigidified')
S='V. Reused typescripts and their distinct proof obligations (038,049,051)'
add('N82',S,'The exponential-of-a-derivation proof needs its coefficient setting',[38],'upper proof and Definition5','unproved claim',r'''The reused typescript proves the multiplicativity of $e^u$ by the Leibniz formula and factorial denominators, but the coefficient-field assumptions lie outside the supplied leaf. Nilpotence makes the sums finite; it does not by itself make the factorials invertible in arbitrary characteristic.''','Adds a separate reused Lie-algebra calculation to CT N66 (39/006), which concerns a formal Hausdorff construction; their shared exponential language does not identify the hypotheses or authorship.','nilpotent|exponential|Hausdorff|Lie')
add('N83',S,'The Levi image claim belongs to the reused source',[38],'last paragraphs, Definitions5–6','unproved claim',r'''“Il est clair” that a special automorphism preserves every ideal, followed by the claim that a surjective image of a Levi subalgebra is Levi, relies on two different mechanisms. The first follows from invariance under $\operatorname{ad}x$ and its exponential, while the second requires the relevant radical and semisimplicity properties under quotient; the page does not give the ambient field or finite-dimensional hypotheses.''','Adds a provenance-qualified comparison to CT N66 (39/006) and differs from CT N01–N05 (36/053–059), whose reused source concerns reductive quotients rather than Levi theory; no new Grothendieck conjecture is inferred.','Levi|radical|Lie|reductive')
add('N84',S,'The correction to left exactness changes the derived-functor setup',[51],'definition of checked H0 and equation14','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The typescript changes “exact à droite” to “exact … gauche” before introducing right derived functors, and replaces section-style formulas by evaluation at $X$. These corrections on051 are mathematically consequential for defining the Čech-cohomology presheaf, even though literal typing remnants remain in the diplomatic text.''','Adds a substantive correction to README S19 and the cohomological-descent lead RS MPL_33_147-181+34_002-017 D21 (33/166:6–18); CT supplies no source-specific comparison for this typescript.','Čech|cohomological descent|foncteur|exact')
add('N85',S,'The injective-presheaf statement has a direct modern comparison',[49,51],'Propositions2.2–2.3; definitions of cohomology presheaves','unpursued implication',r'''The assertion that the inclusion of sheaves sends injectives to injective presheaves has a direct comparison in The Stacks Project (2026c, Lemma 21.10.1, tag 03AV). The typescript’s exact sheafification argument also explains why the associated sheaves of the positive-degree cohomology presheaves vanish; this supports a formal spectral-sequence comparison without identifying a complete edition from two reused leaves.''','Adds an independently checked S19 comparison to RS MPL_33_002-049 §2 and MPL_33_147-181+34_002-017 D21; it differs from CT’s unrelated reused quotient and Haar texts.','Čech|cohomolog|spectral|S19',refs=['P5'])
add('N86',S,'The empty-set convention and T.F. citation remain bibliographic tasks',[49],'opening paragraphs','reading question',r'''The comparison with “[T.F. Chap.II, §5, Th.10.2]” singles out presheaves satisfying $P(\varnothing)=0$ and says that constant presheaves expose a difference. The abbreviated source has not been identified from this leaf, so the exact empty-cover convention and theorem must be recovered before declaring the two Čech theories equivalent.''','Adds a convention-sensitive source task to README S19; CT N01–N02 (36/053,055,059) similarly retain missing definitions or references in reused text, but do not identify T.F.','presheaf|reference|convention|definition')
add('N87',S,'A reused page cannot be dated or attributed from its neighbour',[38,49,51],'printed98/n°316; printed15 and13','reading question',r'''The printed “98”, “n°316”, “15” and “13” mark independent textual sequences, and the mathematical content differs from the handwritten fronts. Their juxtaposition provides evidence of paper reuse, not a secure author, edition, date of drafting, or proof that the handwriting was composed in scan order.''','Adds a source-separation constraint to CT’s title/pagination inventory (36/053,055,057,059,066–072) and bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 §111 and MPL_33_147-181+34_002-017 8.3.','reused|pagination|chronology|printed')
S='VI. Cross-package comparisons and limits of the supplied plan'
add('N88',S,'Filtered duality and arithmetic vanishing need different final steps',[4,33],'weight-filtration remark004; filtered torsion pairing033','own idea',r'''A useful comparison puts the graded lattice–torus/abelian pairings of033 beside the finite-field weight separation reported in CT N40–N41. The first could prove perfectness after graded identifications, whereas the second kills a particular pairing on fixed pieces; moving between them requires a rank and saturation argument, precisely the gap CT N49 already records. This is a proposed bridge to check, not a re-proof of the later orthogonality theorem.''','Adds an explicit link between the current “filtration … poids” and CT N40–N41,N49 (36/075–079); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N97 without re-deriving the prior spectral criterion.','weight|poids|saturation|orthogonal','medium','the filtrations and pairings are structurally compatible, but the comparison of their integral realizations is not supplied')
add('N89',S,'The Artin–Mazur and sign questions suggest a small comparison experiment',[4,7,46],'pairing question004; sign007; finite pairing046','own idea',r'''Fix a single convention for the Poincaré biextension and compute the induced torsion pairing on an elliptic curve, then compare it with the cup-product convention used for the Artin–Mazur construction. Such a calculation could detect a sign or Tate-twist normalization invisible to perfectness alone and supply a preliminary check before the component-group comparison. It would not by itself prove the general compatibility or identify the weighted discriminant form.''','Adds a concrete test prompted by “coïncide” and “au signe près” to CT N55,N57–N59 (36/083–084); bears on RS MPL_36_053-085+39 N57,N59, while leaving their geometric bridge open.','Artin|Mazur|sign|Poincaré','medium','a small example can distinguish normalizations, but a universal comparison needs naturality and degeneration arguments')
add('N90',S,'The supplied subject placement is confirmed but is incomplete',[3,35,47,52],'title and cover sheets; rigidity heading','unpursued implication',r'''“Remarques. SGA7 IX”, “Picard et dualité”, and “biextensions de faisceaux de groupes” confirm the README’s SGA-draft placement. The retained sheets also contain two-term complexes, a tentative higher-Picard duality programme, and unrelated reused Lie/Čech texts, so a monodromy-only description misses substantial content.''','Adds an observed scope correction to README S19 and RS MPL_33_002-049 §2; complements CT’s covers at36/060,073,082 recorded in RS MPL_36_053-085+39 §111.','title|Picard|S19|monodromie')
add('N91',S,'The Hua finding concerns scans deliberately absent from this selection',[3,8,28],'boundary of the retained scan ranges','reading question',r'''The present sequence jumps from008 to028, and the prompt expressly assigns010–027 to another run. Therefore Hua, findings register,36-griffiths-conditional-l-adic, whose cited scans are010–017, cannot be checked against these supplied images; the opening editorial remarks provide no substitute for those missing statements.''','Differs in scope from RS Hua36-griffiths-conditional-l-adic [unsearched conjecture; intake “new to us”]; no confirmation, contradiction, or status change is warranted from these retained scans.','36-griffiths|Griffiths|010|017')
add('N92',S,'Shared obstruction language does not settle the arithmetic register',[31,43,52],'derived class, higher-Picard sketch, rigidity sequence','unpursued implication',r'''The present Ext, torsion and obstruction constructions are structurally suggestive but do not give the image or fibres of the marked depth-two arithmetic section maps. Likewise, none of these sheets states the residue complex or convergence criterion of the class-two germ return, so no proof transfer follows merely from the common word “obstruction”.''','Differs from RS LM7 E.23 [Open, return’s claim], LM7_PUSH C.6 [Proved, return’s claim] and INTAKE LM7_CONTINUED §? [intake, not referee verdict]; adds no change of status or re-derivation of those entries.','LM7 E.23|LM7_PUSH C.6|INTAKE LM7_CONTINUED|section map')
